/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  classifier01_whiten_quartzSlurry

  A CLASSIFIER ON WHITEN'S PARTITION CURVE, CARRYING A LIQUID.  A quartz
  slurry (100 t/h of solids in 57 t/h of water) is split by the generic
  `classifier` unit on the `whiten` corrected curve,

      c(d) = 1 - (e^a - 1) / (e^(a d/d50c) + e^a - 2)

  (Nageswararao, Wiseman & Napier-Munn 2004, eq. 21, p. 681, written there
  for the overflow; the engine divides by the water split and complements
  it), with a bypass R to the coarse product, a_k = R + (1 - R) c_k, and a
  DECLARED fraction of the water leaving with the coarse product.

  WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES: the `whiten` curve's SHAPE, and the classifier's
  fluid branch (`fluidToCoarse`).  The curve's only built-in check is the
  identity c(d50c) = 1/2, which holds for ANY curve through u = a at
  d = d50c; this case holds the WHOLE curve instead.  The solids split
  Y = SUM a_k w_k, the cut sizes read off the grid, the imperfection and the
  coarse product's water are recomputed by an independent closed-form
  script (verification/whitenClosedForm.py, Python standard library, not
  transcribed from the C++), and those values are the hand-written rows at
  the top of `expected`, held at 1e-6.  Reshaping the curve while keeping
  c(d50c) = 1/2 moves them, and the case fails.

  WHAT ITS NUMBERS ARE NOT: a validation.  a = 3.0, d50c = 200 um, R = 0.30
  and the water split 0.35 are ILLUSTRATIVE values chosen for this case
  (the paper says a is "determined by test work" for each feed, p. 681), and
  no measured partition is compared.  The transcription of eq. 21 from the
  paper was not re-read for this case (the paper is not in the tree): the
  form checked here is the one the engine and the design record
  (docs/design/c361-solids-processing.md 7.3) state, so a transcription
  error shared by both would pass.  UNCHECKED: eq. 21 against the paper.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
application   choupoSolve;
description   "A size classifier on Whiten's partition curve (a = 3, d50c = 200 um, bypass 0.30) splitting a quartz slurry, with 35 % of the water declared to the coarse product: the whole curve held to an independent closed-form recomputation, not only its c(d50c) = 1/2 identity.";
verbosity     3;
citations
(
    "Nageswararao, K.; Wiseman, D.M.; Napier-Munn, T.J. (2004) Two empirical hydrocyclone models revisited. Miner. Eng. 17, 671-687, eq. 21, p. 681 (the Whiten form of the efficiency curve)."
    "King, R.P. (2001) Modeling and Simulation of Mineral Processing Systems. Butterworth-Heinemann. Section 4.2, eqs. 4.20-4.29 (classification on size classes)."
);
